# >>> AgentForge >>>
# AgentForge — external-tool credentials (TEMPLATE)
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  HOW TO USE
#    1. Your project has no .env.example of its own: this file is it. Copy it to
#       `.env` in the SAME directory.
#       Your project already has a .env.example: append this whole block, from
#       the `>>> AgentForge >>>` line to the `<<< AgentForge <<<` line, to it.
#       Then add to `.env` only the variable names it does not already have —
#       never overwrite a value your project already sets. A current copy of this
#       block is installed at agentic-assets/agentforge.env.example (ADR-0020).
#    2. Fill in only the systems you actually use — every value is optional and
#       a missing one degrades cleanly (the provider reports "unset", it does not
#       crash and does not silently no-op).
#    3. Load it before running AgentForge commands, e.g.
#         PowerShell:  Get-Content .env | ForEach-Object { if ($_ -match '^\s*([^#][^=]*)=(.*)$') { [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable($Matches[1].Trim(), $Matches[2].Trim()) } }
#         bash:        set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#
#  RULES — these are not suggestions
#    • NEVER commit `.env`. It is gitignored; this `.env.example` is the only
#      file in the pair that belongs in version control, and it must contain
#      PLACEHOLDERS ONLY.
#    • Use a THROWAWAY sandbox for testing — a scratch repo, a free Jira
#      project. Never point these at a customer or production tenant.
#    • Grant the MINIMUM scope that works, and REVOKE the token when finished.
#    • A token pasted into a chat session is exposed to that session. Treat any
#      such token as compromised and revoke it afterwards.
#
#  See docs/guides/how_to_integrate_agentforge_to_external_tools.md for the
#  architecture and the steps to add a provider.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── GitHub ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by the `github` exchange provider (REST API — no `gh` binary required).
# Select it with  "choice": "github"  in exchange_config.json.
#
# Token: a fine-grained PAT is preferred over a classic one.
#   Minimum scopes for outbound push + inbound drive-intent read:
#     • Contents:      Read and write   (publishes exchange.json into the repo)
#     • Issues:        Read and write   (gate-status Issues, drive intents)
#     • Metadata:      Read-only        (implied, required by fine-grained PATs)
#   A classic PAT equivalent is `repo`, which is BROADER than needed — prefer
#   fine-grained and scope it to the single sandbox repository.
# GITHUB_PAT is read as an alias when GITHUB_TOKEN is unset.
GITHUB_TOKEN=YOUR_GITHUB_PAT_HERE

# Target repository as owner/name — e.g. my-org/agentforge-sandbox.
AGENTFORGE_GITHUB_REPO=YOUR_GITHUB_OWNER/YOUR_REPO_HERE


# ── Jira ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT YET IMPLEMENTED as a provider (TD-016). Present so the credential shape is
# documented before the work starts.
#
# NOTE: issue operations and SPRINT/BOARD operations live in DIFFERENT Jira APIs.
# The Cloud REST v3 API covers issues; sprints and boards need the Jira
# Software (Agile) REST API. If you need sprint-level writes, confirm that first.
JIRA_URL=https://YOUR_SITE.atlassian.net
JIRA_EMAIL=YOUR_ATLASSIAN_EMAIL_HERE
JIRA_API_TOKEN=YOUR_ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN_HERE


# ── Confluence ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT YET IMPLEMENTED as a provider (TD-016).
# Same Atlassian token scheme as Jira — one token can serve both.
CONFLUENCE_BASE_URL=https://YOUR_SITE.atlassian.net/wiki
CONFLUENCE_EMAIL=YOUR_ATLASSIAN_EMAIL_HERE
CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN=YOUR_ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN_HERE


# ── Zephyr Scale (test management) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by the QA vendor adapter `ZephyrAdapter` (agentforge/src/qa/zephyr_adapter.py),
# selected with  "choice": "zephyr_scale"  in the QA vendor config and read by
# agentforge/src/qa/vendor_config.py. It uploads JUnit XML to Zephyr Scale's
# automation endpoint (/test-run, /test-plan). It is NOT a drop-in for Jira:
#   • Zephyr's API is SEPARATE from Jira's despite being a Jira app, and needs
#     its own token. Configuring Jira above gives test management nothing.
#   • "Zephyr" is THREE products with different APIs and auth models —
#     Zephyr Scale (formerly TM4J), Zephyr Squad Cloud, and Zephyr Enterprise.
#     ONLY Zephyr Scale is implemented; these variables will not fit the others.
# A missing or malformed base URL is reported as a failed upload, never a crash.
ZEPHYR_BASE_URL=https://api.zephyrscale.smartbear.com/v2
ZEPHYR_API_TOKEN=YOUR_ZEPHYR_API_TOKEN_HERE


# ── TestRail (test management) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by the QA vendor adapter `TestRailAdapter` (agentforge/src/qa/testrail_adapter.py),
# selected with  "choice": "testrail"  in the QA vendor config and read by
# agentforge/src/qa/vendor_config.py. It shells out to TestRail's own CLI
# (`trcli parse_junit`), so `trcli` must be on PATH. It is a pip package, installed
# with the rest by agentic-assets/requirements-agentforge.txt.
TESTRAIL_BASE_URL=https://YOUR_INSTANCE.testrail.io
TESTRAIL_API_KEY=YOUR_TESTRAIL_API_KEY_HERE

# ── Marketing video (/marketing-video, src/neuroedge_marketing) ──────────────
# Offline guide: docs/guides/how_to_create_marketing_video.md
#
# Footage search. Both free; either alone works, both rank better.
PEXELS_API_KEY=YOUR_PEXELS_API_KEY_HERE
PIXABAY_API_KEY=YOUR_PIXABAY_API_KEY_HERE

# Voice-over. OpenAI for drafts, ElevenLabs for sign-off renders. Chatterbox (local) needs no key.
OPENAI_API_KEY=YOUR_OPENAI_API_KEY_HERE
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=YOUR_ELEVENLABS_API_KEY_HERE
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=YOUR_ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID_HERE

# Optional: clip relevance judged by a vision model instead of tags. Anthropic is used when set,
# else OpenAI when OPENAI_API_KEY is set. Costs one call per candidate; `fetch --no-vision` skips it.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=YOUR_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY_HERE
# Leave empty for the default model (claude-haiku-4-5-20251001, or gpt-4o-mini on OpenAI).
NEUROEDGE_VISION_MODEL=

# Optional: where ffmpeg and ffprobe are, when they are not on PATH — their folder, or ffmpeg itself.
FFMPEG_BIN=
# Optional: a .ttf for title-card text, when no usable system font is found (the cards render blank).
DEMO_FONT_FILE=


# ── ML / GenAI model workspace (ai-ml, ai-genai commands) ────────────────────
# Root folder for model development; each objective gets <root>/<intent>-<modality>/.
# Absolute path, not a secret. Unset ⇒ <git root>/neuroedge-ml-projects/ if it exists,
# otherwise the command asks. Rule: agentic-assets/skills/ENGINEERING/_mechanism/ml-artifact-destination.md (ADR-0021).
NEUROEDGE_ML_ROOT=

# ── Hook settings (optional; not secrets) ────────────────────────────────────
# Read by the Node hooks in scripts/hooks/. Hooks see the environment Claude Code
# was started with — a .env file is NOT loaded for them — so set these in that
# shell, or under "env" in .claude/settings.local.json. Left commented because
# none is required.
#
# A command run on every `git commit` Claude makes; the commit is blocked if it
# fails (120 s timeout).
# AGENTFORGE_COMMIT_TEST_CMD=python -m pytest -q
#
# Largest model/dataset file, in bytes, the ML-artifact hook lets a commit add
# (default 26214400 = 25 MB).
# AGENTFORGE_ML_BLOB_CEILING=26214400
#
# One more gate-ledger file for the HITL write hook to protect, besides any gates.json.
# AGENTFORGE_GATES_PATH=
# <<< AgentForge <<<
